# API Reporter – Production OS Prerequisite
## sudoers Configuration for Technical Account `um501001`

---

## 1. Purpose

The API Reporter is deployed on the RCM servers as a Linux `systemd` service:

```text
api-reporter.service
```

During the deployment and validation performed in the **UAT/TE2 environment**, it was identified that the technical account:

```text
um501001
```

was not authorized to execute the privileged `systemctl` operations required to manage the API Reporter service.

The issue was resolved in UAT/TE2 by introducing a restricted configuration under:

```text
/etc/sudoers.d/
```

The same OS prerequisite must therefore be implemented on the Production RCM servers before the API Reporter Production deployment.

---

# 2. Background

The API Reporter runs as a persistent service managed by `systemd`.

Typical operational commands are:

```bash
systemctl status api-reporter
systemctl start api-reporter
systemctl stop api-reporter
systemctl restart api-reporter
systemctl enable api-reporter
```

Operations such as `start`, `stop`, `restart`, and `enable` require elevated OS privileges.

The deployment/operational technical account:

```text
um501001
```

does not have general root privileges and should not be granted unrestricted sudo access.

A dedicated sudoers configuration is therefore required to allow the account to execute only the approved `systemctl` commands for the API Reporter service.

---

# 3. Justification

The requirement was identified during the API Reporter deployment and testing in the **UAT/TE2 environment**.

Without the dedicated sudoers configuration, the technical account cannot perform the required privileged service-management operations.

This could result in a partial deployment where:

```text
RPM successfully installed
        |
        v
Application files deployed
        |
        v
systemctl operation attempted
        |
        v
Permission denied
        |
        v
api-reporter service not correctly started/restarted
```

For this reason, the Production RCM environment should be aligned with the already validated UAT/TE2 configuration before deployment.

---

# 4. Security Principle

The requested configuration follows the **principle of least privilege**.

The account:

```text
um501001
```

must NOT receive unrestricted root access.

The following type of configuration must NOT be used:

```text
um501001 ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL
```

Instead, permission must be restricted to the required operations for:

```text
api-reporter.service
```

For example:

```text
systemctl status api-reporter
systemctl start api-reporter
systemctl stop api-reporter
systemctl restart api-reporter
systemctl enable api-reporter
```

No permission to manage unrelated system services is required.

---

# 5. Recommended Implementation Sequence

The recommended sequence is:

```text
Check existing UAT configuration
        |
        v
Prepare Production sudoers configuration
        |
        v
Validate sudoers syntax
        |
        v
Verify um501001 permissions
        |
        v
Deploy API Reporter
        |
        v
Validate systemd service
        |
        v
Validate API
```

The sudoers prerequisite should preferably be implemented **before the Production deployment**.

---

# 6. Step 1 – Check the Existing UAT/TE2 Configuration

Before implementing the Production configuration, verify the configuration currently used in UAT/TE2.

On the UAT/TE2 RCM server run:

```bash
sudo grep -Rni "um501001\|api-reporter" /etc/sudoers.d/
```

Alternatively:

```bash
sudo grep -Rni "um501001\|api-reporter" \
    /etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.d 2>/dev/null
```

Identify the sudoers file currently used for the API Reporter.

For example:

```text
/etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

Display the configuration:

```bash
sudo cat /etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

The Production configuration should preferably reproduce the configuration already validated in UAT/TE2.

---

# 7. Step 2 – Connect to the Production RCM Server

Connect to the Production RCM server using an administrative account authorized to modify sudoers configuration.

The procedure must be performed on **each Production RCM server hosting the API Reporter**.

For example:

```text
RCM1 Production
RCM2 Production
```

---

# 8. Step 3 – Verify the `systemctl` Path

On the Production server run:

```bash
which systemctl
```

Expected output will normally be:

```text
/bin/systemctl
```

or:

```text
/usr/bin/systemctl
```

Use the **exact path returned by this command** in the sudoers configuration.

Do not assume the path without checking it.

---

# 9. Step 4 – Check for Existing Production Configuration

Before creating a new file, verify whether a sudoers rule already exists.

Run:

```bash
sudo grep -Rni "um501001\|api-reporter" \
    /etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.d 2>/dev/null
```

Also check:

```bash
sudo ls -l /etc/sudoers.d/
```

If an existing API Reporter configuration is found, review it before making any modification.

Avoid creating duplicate or conflicting rules.

---

# 10. Step 5 – Create the Dedicated sudoers File

Create/edit the dedicated file using `visudo`:

```bash
sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

Assuming:

```bash
which systemctl
```

returned:

```text
/bin/systemctl
```

configure:

```text
# ============================================================
# API Reporter service management
# Technical account: um501001
# ============================================================

um501001 ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl status api-reporter
um501001 ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl start api-reporter
um501001 ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl stop api-reporter
um501001 ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl restart api-reporter
```

If the deployment procedure requires `um501001` to enable the service, also add:

```text
um501001 ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl enable api-reporter
```

If `systemctl` is located under:

```text
/usr/bin/systemctl
```

replace `/bin/systemctl` with `/usr/bin/systemctl`.

---

# 11. Step 6 – Configure Ownership

The sudoers file must be owned by `root`.

Run:

```bash
sudo chown root:root /etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

Verify:

```bash
ls -l /etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

---

# 12. Step 7 – Configure File Permissions

Set the standard sudoers permissions:

```bash
sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

Verify:

```bash
ls -l /etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

Expected result:

```text
-r--r----- 1 root root ... /etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

---

# 13. Step 8 – Validate sudoers Syntax

This step is **mandatory**.

Run:

```bash
sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

Expected result:

```text
/etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter: parsed OK
```

If an error is reported:

```text
DO NOT proceed with the Production deployment.
```

Correct the sudoers configuration first and repeat the validation.

It is also possible to validate the complete sudoers configuration:

```bash
sudo visudo -c
```

---

# 14. Step 9 – Verify Permissions Assigned to `um501001`

Run:

```bash
sudo -l -U um501001
```

Verify that the expected API Reporter commands are listed.

For example:

```text
/bin/systemctl status api-reporter
/bin/systemctl start api-reporter
/bin/systemctl stop api-reporter
/bin/systemctl restart api-reporter
```

and, if required:

```text
/bin/systemctl enable api-reporter
```

Verify that the change has NOT introduced unrestricted access such as:

```text
NOPASSWD: ALL
```

---

# 15. Step 10 – Pre-Deployment Functional Test

If `api-reporter.service` already exists on the server, log in as:

```text
um501001
```

and execute:

```bash
sudo systemctl status api-reporter
```

The command should execute without requesting a sudo password and without returning an authorization error.

If the API Reporter has not yet been installed, this functional validation can be performed immediately after deployment.

---

# 16. Step 11 – Proceed with API Reporter Deployment

Once the following checks are successful:

- sudoers file created;
- ownership is `root:root`;
- permissions are `440`;
- `visudo` returns `parsed OK`;
- `sudo -l -U um501001` shows the expected commands;

the normal Production deployment can proceed through the approved deployment mechanism.

For the API Reporter this may involve:

```text
REX
  |
  v
Ansible
  |
  v
RPM deployment
  |
  v
systemd
  |
  v
api-reporter.service
```

---

# 17. Step 12 – Reload systemd if Required

If the deployment installs or modifies:

```text
api-reporter.service
```

reload the systemd configuration:

```bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
```

This instructs systemd to reload its unit definitions.

---

# 18. Step 13 – Enable the API Reporter Service

If required by the deployment procedure:

```bash
sudo systemctl enable api-reporter
```

Verify:

```bash
systemctl is-enabled api-reporter
```

Expected:

```text
enabled
```

If `enable` is performed automatically by the RPM/Ansible deployment, this manual step is not required.

---

# 19. Step 14 – Start or Restart the API Reporter

Start the service if it is not running:

```bash
sudo systemctl start api-reporter
```

For an upgrade/redeployment:

```bash
sudo systemctl restart api-reporter
```

---

# 20. Step 15 – Verify Service Status

Run:

```bash
sudo systemctl status api-reporter
```

Expected:

```text
Active: active (running)
```

The service must not be left in states such as:

```text
inactive
failed
activating
```

---

# 21. Step 16 – Verify the Running Process

Run:

```bash
ps -ef | grep '[a]pi-reporter'
```

Confirm that the expected API Reporter process is running.

---

# 22. Step 17 – Verify the Listening Port

If the API Reporter is configured to listen on TCP port `8000`, run:

```bash
ss -lntp | grep 8000
```

On systems where `ss` is unavailable:

```bash
netstat -lntp | grep 8000
```

The API should normally be listening on the configured local interface, for example:

```text
127.0.0.1:8000
```

---

# 23. Step 18 – Check systemd Logs

If the service does not start correctly, run:

```bash
journalctl -u api-reporter --no-pager -n 100
```

For additional service information:

```bash
systemctl status api-reporter -l
```

These commands should be used to identify startup/runtime errors.

---

# 24. Step 19 – Validate the API Locally

If permitted, verify that the API responds locally from the RCM server.

For example:

```bash
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/reporter
```

The exact endpoint should correspond to the deployed API Reporter configuration.

A successful response confirms that:

```text
systemd
   |
   v
api-reporter.service
   |
   v
Python / FastAPI
   |
   v
Port 8000
```

is functioning correctly.

---

# 25. Step 20 – Validate Apache Proxy Connectivity

After the local API has been validated, verify the external Apache endpoint through the approved Reporter URL.

Architecture:

```text
Consumer
    |
    | HTTPS
    v
Apache httpd :443
    |
    | ProxyPass
    v
127.0.0.1:8000
    |
    v
API Reporter
```

Apache security/mTLS validation is handled separately from the systemd sudoers prerequisite.

---

# 26. If sudoers Is Configured After Deployment

The sudoers configuration can technically be implemented after deployment.

However, the deployment may have already failed during a privileged `systemctl` operation.

The resulting state could be:

```text
RPM installed
     |
     v
Application files installed
     |
     v
systemctl restart attempted
     |
     v
Permission denied
     |
     v
Service stopped/not restarted
```

After implementing and validating `/etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter`, complete the service setup manually.

Run:

```bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
```

If required:

```bash
sudo systemctl enable api-reporter
```

Then:

```bash
sudo systemctl restart api-reporter
```

Finally:

```bash
sudo systemctl status api-reporter
```

Expected:

```text
Active: active (running)
```

Therefore:

> **Preferred:** configure sudoers before deployment.

> **Fallback:** configure sudoers after deployment and manually complete/validate the service activation.

---

# 27. Rollback Procedure

If the sudoers change must be rolled back, first confirm that the file is dedicated exclusively to the API Reporter.

Remove:

```bash
sudo rm /etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

Then validate the complete sudoers configuration:

```bash
sudo visudo -c
```

Removing the sudoers file:

- does not uninstall the API Reporter;
- does not remove the RPM;
- does not automatically stop the running service;
- removes the additional systemctl privileges granted to `um501001`.

After rollback, `um501001` should no longer be able to perform the privileged API Reporter service-management commands.

---

# 28. Expected Final State

After successful implementation:

```text
/etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

exists on each Production RCM server.

The expected state is:

```text
Owner       : root
Group       : root
Permissions : 440
```

Validation:

```bash
sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/api-reporter
```

returns:

```text
parsed OK
```

The technical account:

```text
um501001
```

can execute only the approved API Reporter service-management operations.

The API Reporter service is:

```text
Active: active (running)
```

and:

```bash
systemctl is-enabled api-reporter
```

returns:

```text
enabled
```

